1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,426
Ten jars on the museum shelf.

2
00:00:02,426 --> 00:00:06,561
A sponge, a jellyfish, a tapeworm,
a starfish, and six more.

3
00:00:06,961 --> 00:00:11,781
Each label names a phylum: one big
group of animals sharing the same basic

4
00:00:11,781 --> 00:00:12,681
body plan.

5
00:00:13,125 --> 00:00:16,778
Diya wants to be a doctor,
and NEET asks about every jar.

6
00:00:16,778 --> 00:00:19,901
She asked: is there one
key that sorts them all?

7
00:00:20,251 --> 00:00:21,151
There is.

8
00:00:21,127 --> 00:00:26,504
Ten non-chordate phyla, sorted by the
body features from the last few episodes.

9
00:00:27,004 --> 00:00:31,285
By the end, you will know each phylum
by its key feature and its NCERT

10
00:00:31,285 --> 00:00:32,185
examples.

11
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:34,175
Let's open the jars.

12
00:00:34,525 --> 00:00:35,425
One.

13
00:00:35,061 --> 00:00:36,773
Porifera, the sponges.

14
00:00:36,773 --> 00:00:37,950
Cellular level.

15
00:00:37,950 --> 00:00:42,763
Water flows in through the ostia and
out through the osculum, kept moving

16
00:00:42,763 --> 00:00:44,056
by collar cells.

17
00:00:44,056 --> 00:00:46,998
Examples: Sycon, Spongilla and Euspongia.

18
00:00:47,498 --> 00:00:48,398
Two.

19
00:00:48,073 --> 00:00:50,712
Coelenterata, also called Cnidaria.

20
00:00:50,712 --> 00:00:55,731
Radial and diploblastic, with stinging
cells called cnidoblasts, and two

21
00:00:55,731 --> 00:00:58,687
body forms, the polyp and the medusa.

22
00:00:58,687 --> 00:01:01,901
Examples: Physalia, Adamsia and Meandrina.

23
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:03,301
Three.

24
00:01:03,043 --> 00:01:05,190
Ctenophora, the comb jellies.

25
00:01:05,190 --> 00:01:10,010
They swim with eight rows of ciliated
comb plates, and they can glow.

26
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:12,683
Examples: Pleurobrachia and Ctenoplana.

27
00:01:13,183 --> 00:01:14,083
Four.

28
00:01:13,736 --> 00:01:15,790
Platyhelminthes, the flatworms.

29
00:01:15,790 --> 00:01:20,490
Flattened from top to bottom, acoelomate,
with flame cells for excretion.

30
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:24,727
Examples: Taenia, the tapeworm,
and Fasciola, the liver fluke.

31
00:01:25,227 --> 00:01:26,127
Five.

32
00:01:25,835 --> 00:01:28,041
Aschelminthes, the roundworms.

33
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:33,149
Round in cross-section and pseudocoelomate,
with separate males and females.

34
00:01:33,149 --> 00:01:36,464
Examples: Ascaris,
Wuchereria and Ancylostoma.

35
00:01:36,964 --> 00:01:37,864
Six.

36
00:01:37,488 --> 00:01:38,388
Annelida.

37
00:01:38,269 --> 00:01:43,204
Metameric and coelomate, with closed
circulation and nephridia for excretion.

38
00:01:43,204 --> 00:01:48,180
Examples: Nereis, Pheretima, the
earthworm, and Hirudinaria, the leech.

39
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:49,580
Seven.

40
00:01:49,345 --> 00:01:51,680
Arthropoda, the largest phylum.

41
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:54,745
A chitin exoskeleton
and jointed appendages.

42
00:01:54,745 --> 00:02:00,100
Examples: Apis, the honey bee,
Anopheles, the mosquito, and Limulus, the

43
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:02,282
king crab, a living fossil.

44
00:02:02,782 --> 00:02:03,682
Eight.

45
00:02:03,434 --> 00:02:06,056
Mollusca, the second largest phylum.

46
00:02:06,056 --> 00:02:10,966
A soft body with a muscular foot,
often inside a shell, and a file-like

47
00:02:10,966 --> 00:02:12,988
rasping organ, the radula.

48
00:02:12,988 --> 00:02:16,316
Examples: Pila, Pinctada,
Sepia and Octopus.

49
00:02:16,816 --> 00:02:17,716
Nine.

50
00:02:17,422 --> 00:02:20,226
Echinodermata, the spiny-skinned animals.

51
00:02:20,226 --> 00:02:24,784
All marine, with a water vascular
system for moving and feeding.

52
00:02:24,784 --> 00:02:28,758
Examples: Asterias, the
starfish, Echinus and Ophiura.

53
00:02:29,258 --> 00:02:30,158
Ten.

54
00:02:29,809 --> 00:02:30,846
Hemichordata.

55
00:02:30,846 --> 00:02:35,900
A worm-like body in three parts,
proboscis, collar and trunk, with a rod

56
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,504
in the collar called the stomochord.

57
00:02:38,504 --> 00:02:41,355
Examples: Balanoglossus and Saccoglossus.

58
00:02:42,005 --> 00:02:44,656
Now the key: a few yes or no questions.

59
00:02:45,006 --> 00:02:47,249
Cells only, with no tissues?

60
00:02:47,249 --> 00:02:48,149
Porifera.

61
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,426
Two germ layers?

62
00:02:49,426 --> 00:02:51,767
Stinging cells mean Coelenterata.

63
00:02:51,767 --> 00:02:53,835
Comb plates mean Ctenophora.

64
00:02:54,285 --> 00:02:57,059
Three germ layers but no body cavity?

65
00:02:57,059 --> 00:02:58,280
Platyhelminthes.

66
00:02:58,280 --> 00:03:00,702
A false cavity, a pseudocoelom?

67
00:03:00,702 --> 00:03:01,814
Aschelminthes.

68
00:03:02,264 --> 00:03:04,816
A true coelom and a segmented body?

69
00:03:04,816 --> 00:03:05,716
Annelida.

70
00:03:05,617 --> 00:03:08,485
Add jointed legs, and it is Arthropoda.

71
00:03:08,935 --> 00:03:11,213
A true coelom with no segments?

72
00:03:11,213 --> 00:03:14,176
A water vascular system
means Echinodermata.

73
00:03:14,176 --> 00:03:16,390
A muscular foot means Mollusca.

74
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:19,564
Proboscis, collar and
trunk mean Hemichordata.

75
00:03:20,214 --> 00:03:21,315
Remember this.

76
00:03:21,315 --> 00:03:25,278
Every non-chordate phylum has
one feature that gives it away.

77
00:03:25,878 --> 00:03:27,883
Two pairs get mixed up the most.

78
00:03:28,233 --> 00:03:32,495
Coelenterates and ctenophores
are both radial and diploblastic.

79
00:03:32,495 --> 00:03:37,151
But coelenterates carry stinging
cnidoblasts, and ctenophores swim with

80
00:03:37,151 --> 00:03:38,216
comb plates.

81
00:03:38,666 --> 00:03:41,414
Flatworms and annelids are both worm-like.

82
00:03:41,414 --> 00:03:45,907
But flatworms are flat, unsegmented
and acoelomate, while annelids are

83
00:03:45,907 --> 00:03:48,127
segmented, with a true coelom.

84
00:03:48,727 --> 00:03:49,627
Your turn.

85
00:03:49,623 --> 00:03:53,105
A leech sucks blood, like
many parasitic flatworms.

86
00:03:53,105 --> 00:03:55,546
Is it a flatworm, or an annelid?

87
00:03:55,546 --> 00:03:56,864
Pause and decide.

88
00:04:00,064 --> 00:04:00,964
Annelid!

89
00:04:00,750 --> 00:04:05,366
Hirudinaria, the leech, is segmented
and has a true coelom, just like the

90
00:04:05,366 --> 00:04:06,266
earthworm.

91
00:04:06,798 --> 00:04:10,160
Now, a worked example: a
jar labelled cuttlefish.

92
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:11,294
Is it a fish?

93
00:04:11,644 --> 00:04:14,223
Tissues and organs, not loose cells.

94
00:04:14,223 --> 00:04:15,272
Not Porifera.

95
00:04:15,722 --> 00:04:18,122
Three germ layers and a true coelom.

96
00:04:18,122 --> 00:04:20,173
Not a flatworm or a roundworm.

97
00:04:20,623 --> 00:04:24,294
No segments, no water vascular
system, and no notochord.

98
00:04:24,294 --> 00:04:27,151
So it is not a chordate, and not a fish.

99
00:04:27,601 --> 00:04:30,625
A soft body, a muscular
foot, and a radula.

100
00:04:30,625 --> 00:04:32,358
It is Sepia, a mollusc.

101
00:04:33,008 --> 00:04:34,474
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:34,824 --> 00:04:39,380
Many students place Hemichordata under
the chordates, because of the stomochord

103
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:40,461
in its collar.

104
00:04:40,861 --> 00:04:44,094
But the stomochord only
looks like a notochord.

105
00:04:44,094 --> 00:04:49,268
Hemichordata was once a sub-phylum of
Chordata, and is now a separate non-chordate

106
00:04:49,268 --> 00:04:50,168
phylum.

107
00:04:50,611 --> 00:04:52,028
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:52,028 --> 00:04:56,338
As match questions that pair a phylum
with its feature or its example.

109
00:04:56,938 --> 00:04:58,144
Exam level now.

110
00:04:58,144 --> 00:05:00,557
Match each phylum with its feature.

111
00:05:00,557 --> 00:05:01,815
Pause and solve.

112
00:05:07,215 --> 00:05:08,481
The answer is C.

113
00:05:08,981 --> 00:05:13,355
Comb plates belong to ctenophores,
and flame cells to flatworms.

114
00:05:13,855 --> 00:05:18,970
The radula is in molluscs, and the
water vascular system is in echinoderms.

115
00:05:19,570 --> 00:05:21,026
Here is your recap.

116
00:05:21,026 --> 00:05:22,523
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:25,723 --> 00:05:28,721
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:05:29,121 --> 00:05:30,021
One.

119
00:05:29,650 --> 00:05:32,312
Which phylum has a water canal system?

120
00:05:33,212 --> 00:05:34,112
Two.

121
00:05:33,778 --> 00:05:36,329
Name two examples of Aschelminthes.

122
00:05:37,229 --> 00:05:38,129
Three.

123
00:05:37,805 --> 00:05:40,829
Why is the leech an
annelid and not a flatworm?

124
00:05:41,929 --> 00:05:46,739
Next time: a fish embryo and a human
embryo share a rod along the back.

125
00:05:46,739 --> 00:05:48,492
What else do they share?

126
00:05:48,492 --> 00:05:51,107
We find out in chordate characteristics.
